WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.080
Good morning John!

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:02.320
Who the heck are you?

00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:03.120
Who the heck am I?

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:04.280
How do I know who I am?

00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:05.620
Do other people know who I am?

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:07.200
Do other people have different ideas of who I am?

00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:09.100
Are those ideas close together or far apart?

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:12.480
And what is more me, the me in their heads or the me in mine?

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:15.260
And when I go to sleep at night, and lose consciousness for eight hours,

00:00:15.260 --> 00:00:19.200
and wake up, am I still the same me that I was when I went to bed?!

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:20.380
Who am I?

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:22.020
Hi. My name is Hank Green.

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:24.400
I'm a heterosexual, white, cis male.

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:26.240
I'm a Taurus and an ENFP.

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:29.960
I'm a Montanan, an American, a book lover. I don't have any tattoos,

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:33.040
and I think that climate change is the greatest challenge that humanity has ever faced.

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:37.060
I'm an entrepreneur, a YouTuber, a Slytherclaw, and I am Divergent.

00:00:37.060 --> 00:00:41.580
I once took a Buzzfeed quiz to tell me what YouTuber I was, in which I was one of the options,

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:43.420
and I came up, you guessed it:

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:44.380
Miranda Sings

00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:47.160
Thanks for the identity crisis, Buzzfeed.

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:51.760
I am now 35, and based on what I thought about 35-year-olds when I was 25,

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:55.120
and when I was 15, I should know who the frick I am.

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:58.840
Nevertheless, I don't. I can describe myself all day long.

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:02.960
I can take personality tests that validate my behavior, until the cows have baby cows,

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:07.380
and then those baby cows have their own baby cows and those third-generation cows finally come home.

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:11.240
But that won't change the fact that myself is just a story that I tell to myself

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:17.760
that is just like any other story: subjective, and incomplete, and at least a little bit of a lie.

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:21.680
I got my way of seeing the world, I got my values, I got the stuff that I care about, the people I care about.

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:23.360
All those things, they're important, they're real

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:24.400
But where do they come from?

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:26.900
Certainly not things that I was born with.

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:28.640
I am me, right now.

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:30.600
I have not always been this me.

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:35.500
Throughout my life, who I am has been largely dependent upon what people expect of me.

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:37.380
There is a truly weird and unexplained link

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:39.680
between bad eyesight and intelligence.

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:41.920
One of the theories that explains this and my favorite

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:43.420
is that kids who wear glasses

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:47.240
look smarter to their peers, and to their parents, and to their teachers,

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:50.280
and those people then expect them to be smart,

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:52.600
and then those kids are smart.

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:55.500
They just fulfill those expectations and become smart.

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:58.180
It's certainly well-known that the belief that a child is intelligent

00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:00.660
will result in that child becoming more intelligent,

00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:03.620
even if the original belief was falsely planted there.

00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:06.320
So like now I have a bunch of people who watch me make videos,

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:09.040
and they respect me, and-and trust me.

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:12.880
And so I try to become a person worthy of that trust and respect.

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:13.920
But not all the time.

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:15.840
Sometimes I'm down to clown,

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:20.280
Sometimes I'm introspective and I just want to stare at the wall and sing.

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:22.260
I crave consistency in myself,

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:25.140
but myself denies me that simplicity.

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:29.060
So we look to books and Buzzfeed quizzes and personality tests and horoscopes

00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:30.660
to tell us who we might be.

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:33.000
To see what others might think, to tell us

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:35.500
that we are doing a good job of becoming the kind of person

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:36.280
we want to be.

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:39.340
And to tell us that it's okay to be the kind of person that you are

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:42.060
but all of these things are such pale reflections

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:45.720
of the indescribably weird and complex thing that is the self

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:47.400
Identity is important to try to define,

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:52.760
but there isn't some perfect, Platonic ideal of what you is buried beneath all of the gunk.

00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:55.340
Figuring out who you are, at least at this particular moment,

00:02:55.340 --> 00:02:57.420
Is all about trying to tease out

00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:01.180
which bits are you, trying to fill other people's expectations for your whole life,

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:03.040
what bits you want to change to make yourself better,

00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:04.700
and what bits you cherish

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:06.580
as vital to your you-ness.

00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:10.260
But people who are like, "Be yourself," and they expect that to mean something.

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:13.680
That is not advice, that's an existential crisis waiting too happen.

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:15.900
These tools that we use to help us define ourselves,

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:18.380
to help us figure out who we are, they are fine as tools.

00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:21.460
But they are not walls to box ourselves in with.

00:03:21.460 --> 00:03:25.640
You will wake up one day, and you will not be the you you once were.

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:27.620
And that will keep happening to you until you die,

00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:28.740
which is kind of wonderful.

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:32.780
Why live life as just one person, when you can be so many different people?

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:34.000
A little scarier perhaps,

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:36.360
but as with a lot of things that scare me,

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:38.460
turns out, also pretty fun.

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:40.428
John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

